A topic definition is three facts in one constant: the name, the payload it carries and
the lane that carries it. Every end of the topic is opened from that constant, so no
program states any of the three a second time, and two programs that import one
definition cannot disagree about them. The frame a payload's numbers are in belongs to the
topic, and its doc comment names it.

`Range` and `Twist` are standard payloads, from the crate `robot_messages`: one definition
of each, which C (`<tail/messages.h>`) and Python (`tail_messages`) are generated from, so
a C or Python node at the other end holds the same one. A payload of your own is a struct
with `#[derive(WirePlain)]`; see [Payloads](#payloads). No program here writes `unsafe`.

Reads a sensor every 20 ms and publishes the reading, stamped with the time it was taken.
This is the whole program:

```rust
// app/lidar/src/main.rs

#[path = "../../robot_topics.rs"]
mod robot_topics;

use periodic::{periodic, Out};
use robot_topics::{Range, SENSOR_RANGE};

#[periodic(every = "20ms", on_overrun = Skip)]
fn lidar(#[publish(SENSOR_RANGE)] range: Out<Range>) {
    range.publish(Range {
        stamp_ns: periodic::monotonic_ns(),
        radiation_type: Range::INFRARED,
        field_of_view: 0.05,
        min_range: 0.02,
        max_range: 8.0,
        range: 4.2,
        variance: 0.0,
    });
}
```

`periodic::monotonic_ns()` is the stamp clock: nanoseconds since boot on the counter the
node's releases are placed on. `Range::INFRARED` is one of the payload's named values.

The lidar stamps with `monotonic_ns()` because it is at the hardware boundary: the stamp is
the instant it read the device. A node that decides something from its inputs stamps with
`periodic::now()` instead -- the current release's one clock reading, in nanoseconds, the
instant every input of the release was judged against; 0 before the first release -- or
copies an input's `stamp_ns`. A replay of the node (below, "Recording and replay") hands the
body the recorded `now()`, so what it stamps replays exactly; `monotonic_ns()` reads the
counter as the body runs, which no recording holds.

Reads that topic every 20 ms and brakes on it. `stale_after` says how old a reading may be
before it counts as absent, so the fail-safe branch is the one that runs when the sensor
goes quiet:

