A release on which the body does not call `publish` publishes nothing on that output: the
topic's sequence does not advance, its readers keep the previous value until their
`stale_after` says it is too old, and the release is **not** an overrun nor a refused
publish. An `Out` the body never touches on any path is an `unused_variables` warning;
name it `_range` if that is meant.

What the value costs. Measured on nodes that publish a 65 536-byte payload on the safe
lane, built for `aarch64-unknown-tail` in release and disassembled (`make ci-fill-in-place`;
built, not run):

| The output is published | Largest stack frame holding the payload |
|---|---|
| on every release | none: the value is built in the shared element (largest frame in the program: 8 272 bytes, in `std::io::copy`; `main` is 1 328) |
| on every other release | none: built in the shared element as well (largest frame 8 272 bytes) |
| two outputs, the second on every other release | none: each built in its own element (largest frame 8 272 bytes) |

A release that publishes nothing on an output costs nothing on the stack either. Limit:
these hold only while the compiler inlines the body into the generated `main`; a body marked
`#[inline(never)]` holds its payload on the stack (TAIL-490). On the kernel lane a
publication is always copied, into a slot of the topic in the kernel, and a kernel-lane
payload is at most 4 KiB.

