Each output is one parameter and one topic, and each is published or not on its own. They
are published when the body ends, in the order they are declared, wherever they stand
among the other parameters; a served action's status goes after all of them. A refusal on
one output does not stop the others, and is counted and reported for that output alone
(see [`NodeError`](#nodeerror)).

| | What happens |
|---|---|
| the body publishes `state` before `command` | both are published in declaration order: `command`, then `state` |
| the body panics after `command.publish(..)` | nothing is published, on either topic |
| `command`'s lane refuses on 16 releases in a row | one ERROR record naming `cmd/velocity`; `state` goes on being published |
| the node cannot start after creating `cmd/velocity` and `drive/state` | one ERROR record for the reason, then one per output left without a writer, in order |

An output is declared, not created, and `publish` sends nothing at the call: what the
body hands its outputs is published at the end of the release, so a half-run release
publishes nothing.

