`Permit` is `pub struct Permit { pub issuer: u32 }`, and `DRIVE_PERMIT` a
`Topic<Permit, SafeLane>`.

Why this shape:

- **One branch for every failure of a component.** A component that is late, lost, or
  never came up is not usable, so no permit is published. The monitor asks `get()` and
  nothing else: no latching stop, no health topics, no `was_stale()`.
- **One branch for every failure of the monitor.** A monitor that cannot start, is stopped
  by `Fault`, or is starved of the processor is silence on `drive/permit`, which the drive
  node already acts on as it acts on any absent input.
- **Start-up order does not matter.** Before the monitor has seen all three topics it
  publishes nothing, and the drive node stays stopped.

The drive node's bound on the permit follows the [sizing rule](#sizing-stale_after): one
lost permit tolerated, a 50 ms monitor and a 20 ms drive node give `(1 + 1) x 50 + 20 =
120 ms`, written half a period off the drive node's grid as `"130ms"`.

What the drive node does, release by release, against scripted peers: a planner that
sends two commands and dies, a range sensor that goes quiet once and once sees an
obstacle, and a monitor that stops permitting for a while. Ages are since receipt at this
node.

```rust
use periodic::{periodic, Cycle, Out};
use robot_topics::{Imu, Odometry, Pose, ESTIMATE_POSE, IMU_DATA, WHEEL_ODOMETRY};

#[periodic(every = "10ms", on_overrun = Skip, init = Pose::default())]
fn estimator(
    pose: &mut Pose,
    #[subscribe(IMU_DATA, stale_after = "35ms")] imu: Cycle<Imu>,
    #[subscribe(WHEEL_ODOMETRY, stale_after = "65ms")] odometry: Cycle<Odometry>,
    #[publish(ESTIMATE_POSE)] estimate: Out<Pose>,
) {
    let Some(imu) = imu.get() else { return }; // no usable inertial reading: publish nothing
    pose.heading_mrad += imu.yaw_rate_mrad_s / 100;
    if let (true, Some(wheels)) = (odometry.arrived(), odometry.get()) {
        pose.x_mm = wheels.travelled_mm; // applied once, on the release it arrives
        pose.corrections += 1;
    }
    estimate.publish(*pose);
}
```

An input is the **newest** publication on its topic. Publications that arrived since the
previous release and were passed over for a newer one are not shown. So:

- **A topic read by an input carries state**: a total, a pose, a command. `Odometry` here
  is the distance travelled since power-on, not since the last reading, so a total that
  was passed over is contained in the next.
- **A topic that carries increments must not be read by an input.** Of two increments
  published inside one of the reader's periods, the first is passed over.
- **`arrived()` applies a measurement once.** `get()` keeps answering with the same total
  for as long as it stays usable; `arrived()` is true only on the release that received
  it.

Measured on QEMU raspi3b (`periodic_estimator`, five runs): a node every 20 ms reading a
total on the kernel lane. Eight totals published one at a time were each applied exactly
once, with the node seen to run at least three more releases with the total still usable.
Five bursts of six totals published back to back each ended on the burst's last total, in
one correction per burst.

