#[path = "../../robot_topics.rs"]
mod robot_topics;

use periodic::{periodic, Cycle, Out};
use robot_frames::{BASE_LINK, ODOM, RANGE_FRONT, ROVER};
use robot_topics::{Odometry, Point, Range, OBSTACLE_IN_ODOM, ODOMETRY, SENSOR_RANGE};

// The same obstacle in odom: nothing is published while odometry is absent or stale.
#[periodic(every = "20ms", on_overrun = Skip)]
fn obstacle_in_odom(
    #[subscribe(SENSOR_RANGE, stale_after = "70ms")] range: Cycle<Range>,
    #[subscribe(ODOMETRY, stale_after = "70ms")] odometry: Cycle<Odometry>,
    #[publish(OBSTACLE_IN_ODOM)] obstacle: Out<Point>,
) {
    let frames = ROVER.with(BASE_LINK, odometry.get().map(|o| &o.pose));
    let Ok(ranger_in_odom) = frames.lookup(ODOM, RANGE_FRONT) else { return };
    let Some(point) = range.get().and_then(Range::point) else { return };
    obstacle.publish(ranger_in_odom.transform_point(point));
}
```

`with(BASE_LINK, ..)` supplies the moving link's pose for this release, borrowed from the
input. Its age limit is the input's `stale_after`, applied by `Cycle::get()`: the
framework's one freshness rule, with no second clock in `frames`. Every lookup that crosses
the link is then one of:

| Supplied | `lookup(ODOM, RANGE_FRONT)` |
|---|---|
| `None`: odometry absent, or older than 70 ms | `Err(Absent { frame: "BASE_LINK", parent: "ODOM" })`, printed `no usable pose of base_link in odom` |
| a NaN or infinite position, or an orientation 1 % or more from unit length (all zero included) | `Err(NotAPose { .. })`, printed `the pose of base_link in odom is not a rigid transform` |
| a finite position and an orientation less than 1 % from unit length | `Ok`: the orientation normalised to unit length, then composed |

So when odometry stops, `obstacle_in_odom` publishes nothing while `obstacle` goes on. With
`base_link` at (1, 2, 0) m in `odom`, turned 90 degrees left, the obstacle is at
(-0.0870399400647699, 6.156888286178356, 0) in `odom`. A second `.with(..)` supplies a
second moving link; a second value for one link replaces the first. Because the view
borrows the input, it cannot outlive the release that judged the input fresh.

Run until the policy stops the node, then return the releases the stopping cycle ran past.
The closure receives each cycle's overrun count. Only `Fault` stops a node, so under `Skip`
or `CatchUp` this never returns.

The closure's count and the returned one never overlap. Under `Skip` and `CatchUp` every
overrun reaches the next cycle's closure. Under `Fault` the first overrun is the stop, so
the closure only ever sees zero and the return value is the one place that count goes —
which is why it is `#[must_use]`. A hand-built executor logs neither, so report both, as
the attribute does:

```rust
let mut executor = Executor::new(clock, Duration::from_millis(20), policy)?;
let missed = executor.run(|overruns| {
    if overruns > 0 { log::log!(WARNING, "lidar: missed {overruns} release(s)"); }
    do_work();
});
// Only Fault gets here.
log::log!(ERROR, "lidar: stopped: missed {missed} release(s) under on_overrun = Fault");
std::process::exit(1);
```

Run one cycle. Returns `std::ops::ControlFlow`: `Continue(())` to run the next, or
`Break(missed)` once the policy has stopped the node — only `Fault` does — carrying the
same count `run` returns, and `#[must_use]` for the same reason.

```rust
let missed = loop {
    if let ControlFlow::Break(missed) = executor.step(|_overruns| do_work()) {
        break missed;
    }
};
